From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:20:13 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] wireplumber: keep the headphone volume and mute of every
 Mac across boots

The "Built-in Audio Headphones" sink of the M1 and M2 Macs came back at
100 % and unmuted after every boot, whatever the user had set on it.

With nothing in the jack, the headphone route is unavailable. WirePlumber
then selects no route for the headphone output, so its node reports the
ALSA layer's initial volume, and a volume or mute set on it goes nowhere.
asahi-unplugged-routes.lua already selected the unplugged route for the
J700 only. Even with the route selected, WirePlumber's state-routes.lua
skips storing the props of a route that is unavailable, so a mute set
with nothing plugged in was still lost at the next start.

Enable asahi.routes.select-unplugged for the built-in card of every Mac
(the macaudio cards are all named "Mac..."), and keep the J700's own
-60 dB default volume as a separate rule.

In asahi-unplugged-routes.lua, store the props a client asks to save on
an unavailable route of such a device, in a state file of its own
(asahi-unplugged-routes, keyed like default-routes), and apply them in
place of WirePlumber's stored props whenever that route is selected. A
change made with headphones plugged in is stored by WirePlumber as usual
and drops the record. Both follow device.restore-routes, like
WirePlumber's own route state.

Tested on the M2 (J413) and the M1 Pro (J316), WirePlumber 0.5.18: a
mute set on the unplugged headphone sink is stored, and a WirePlumber
restart brings the sink back muted at the stored volume.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
